countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Denmark’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9433"} "Erik Hansen"] " was the reigning Olympic, European, and World Champion in the K-1 1000, making him a strong favorite to defend his title at the 1964 Summer Games. The only major tournament in which he had been bested was the 1961 European Championships, where he placed third behind " [:a {:href "/athletes/10808"} "Carl von Gerber"] " of Sweden and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10601"} "Vasile Nicoară"] " of Romania, neither of whom were competing in the K-1 1000 in Tokyo. Instead, their nations were represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/10799"} "Rolf Peterson"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10627"} "Aurel Vernescu"] " respectively, the latter of whom was the current European and World runner-up. Non-Olympian Siegfried Rossberg was the most recent European and World bronze medalist, but Germany competed as a unified nation for the final time in 1964 and sent West German " [:a {:href "/athletes/9988"} "Erich Suhrbier"] " instead. The only other major medalist in the tournament was the Soviet Union’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/11003"} "Igor Pisarev"] ", who had been second in the event in 1956. Hungary, the defending Olympic runner-up, sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/10067"} "Mihály Hesz"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "In the opening round, the first heat was the most competitive and saw Hansen win it in the fastest time, ahead of Hesz and Peterson. Vernescu bested Suhrbier in the second heat, while Pisarev finished third behind Poland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/10528"} "Władysław Szuszkiewicz"] " and Great Britain’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9793"} "Alistair Wilson"] " in a heat where everyone advanced. Szuszkiewicz, in a heat that included Hesz and was won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/9083"} "Günther Pfaff"] " of Austria, was the only one of these men not to advance after the semi-finals. Vernescu and Hansen won the other heats, the latter once again with the round’s fastest time. Considering his performance thus far, Hansen seemed as if he was a shoo-in for gold, but he struggled heavily in the final and finished seventh. In its closing seconds, the race for the Olympic title came down to a drive between Peterson and Hesz, the former of whom edged out the latter by only 0.15 seconds. Hungary thus settled for silver while Vernescu came in shortly thereafter for bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Rep"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "132"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10799"} "Rolf Peterson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SWE.png"}] "SWE"]] [:td {} "4:04.27 (3 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:06.97 (2 h2)"] [:td {} "3:57.13 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "68"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10067"} "Mihály Hesz"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} "4:02.90 (2 h1)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:04.57 (2 h3)"] [:td {} "3:57.28 (2)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "127"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10627"} "Aurel Vernescu"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ROU"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ROU.png"}] "ROU"]] [:td {} "4:02.06 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "4:03.35 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "4:00.77 (3)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"